anyone an explanation for which you picked.

## Why two

Because the right answer depends on who is asking, and only you know that.

**Apache-2.0 grants patent rights explicitly** (§3) and adds a retaliation
clause. MIT says nothing about patents — the implied licence doctrine
generally covers it, but "generally" is exactly the word that stalls a legal
review. Public-sector and large-enterprise counsel often want the explicit
grant, so Apache-2.0 removes a question from their checklist.

**MIT is compatible with everything, including GPLv2.** Apache-2.0 is not
compatible with GPLv2 (it is fine with GPLv3). If GPLv2 code is anywhere in
your dependency tree, MIT is the side you want.

Neither licence alone serves both readers. Offering both costs nothing and
means nobody has to argue with their lawyer about it. This is the same
arrangement the Rust ecosystem uses, for the same reason.

